  "title": "Simultaneous Equations (Linear)",
  "content": "<p><strong>Simultaneous equations</strong> are two equations with two unknowns. You find values of both variables that satisfy both equations at the same time.</p><p><strong>Elimination method:</strong> Make the coefficients of one variable the same (by multiplying one or both equations). Then add or subtract to eliminate that variable. Solve for the remaining variable, then substitute back.</p><p><strong>Substitution method:</strong> Rearrange one equation to make one variable the subject, then substitute into the other equation.</p><p>Remember: if the coefficients have the <em>same sign</em>, subtract the equations. If they have <em>different signs</em>, add them.</p>",
  "example": "<p><strong>Solve</strong> \\(2x + y = 7\\) and \\(3x - y = 8\\)</p><p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Add: \\(5x = 15\\) → \\(x = 3\\)</p><p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Sub into (1): \\(6 + y = 7\\) → \\(y = 1\\)</p><p><strong>Answer:</strong> \\(x = 3, y = 1\\)</p>"
 },
